## Current roadmap / platform inheritance

GitHub #78 and `docs/MESSENGER_PLATFORM_ARCHITECTURE.md` are current authority. Existing domain/security rules remain valid.

Messenger surfaces require abuse controls for messaging, discovery, uploads, Channels/Groups and realtime connections. New social functionality must integrate #104 block/report semantics where applicable and bound WebSocket/concurrent connection/message/media costs.


# Abuse-control rules

Use layered controls. User allowance alone is not an abuse-control system.

Apply relevant combinations of:
- per-IP rate limits;
- per-user/account rate limits;
- auth/OTP resend and attempt limits;
- per-action concurrency limits;
- request/body/context/file size limits;
- per-request provider-cost caps;
- per-user rolling provider-cost caps;
- global provider spend/burn circuit breakers;
- queue depth/concurrency limits.

Rules:
- Limits are centralized/configurable, not scattered literals.
- Critical AI/payment/auth limits are server-enforced.
- Redis may coordinate counters/leases but never stores authoritative money.
- If production rate/concurrency coordination is unavailable for expensive operations, prefer fail-closed/503 over unlimited provider spend.
- Avoid retry storms and synchronized retries; use bounded backoff/jitter where retry is safe.
- Log abuse/security signals without storing secrets or full sensitive payloads.
